- {
    "id": "magnetic_vector_operator",
    "name": "Magnetic anomaly from magnetization",
    "inputs": [
      "magnetization",
      "inducing_magnetic_field"
    ],
    "outputs": [
      "magnetic_anomaly"
    ],
    "relation_class": "observation_operator",
    "label": "ΔT = (μ₀/4π) Ĥ₀·∫∇∇(1/|r−r′|)·M dV′",
    "equation": "\\\\Delta T_B(\\\\mathbf r)\\\\approx\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf B(\\\\mathbf r)=\\\\frac{\\\\mu_0}{4\\\\pi}\\\\,\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0\\\\cdot\\\\int_{\\\\Omega}\\\\nabla\\\\nabla\\\\!\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathbf r-\\\\mathbf r'|}\\\\right)\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf M(\\\\mathbf r')\\\\,dV'\\\\;\\\\equiv\\\\;(G_M\\\\mathbf M)(\\\\mathbf r)",
    "variables": {
      "magnetization": "\\\\mathbf M",
      "inducing_magnetic_field": "\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0",
      "magnetic_anomaly": "\\\\Delta T_B"
    },
    "assumptions": [
      "Observation points outside the magnetized volume; magnetostatics (no conduction currents), so $\\\\mathbf B=\\\\mu_0\\\\mathbf H$ there and the anomalous field derives from a scalar potential.",
      "Total-field linearisation $|\\\\mathbf B|\\\\ll|\\\\mathbf B_0|$: the measured anomaly is the projection $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf B$ onto the ambient direction. $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0$ (IGRF for the site and date) is assumed known and uniform over the survey.",
      "$G_M$ is this linear integral operator with the dipole kernel $\\\\tfrac{\\\\mu_0}{4\\\\pi}\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf H}_0\\\\cdot\\\\nabla\\\\nabla|\\\\mathbf r-\\\\mathbf r'|^{-1}$; the executable form is its discretisation over prism cells (Li & Oldenburg 1996, eq. 1, with $\\\\mathbf M=\\\\chi\\\\mathbf H_0$ absorbed), one dense row per datum.",
      "Vector magnetization may include remanence; with induced-only magnetization the two steps collapse to a single matrix acting on $\\\\chi$."
    ],
    "forward": {
      "type": "explicit",
      "unique": true,
      "difficulty": "low"
    },
    "inverse": {
      "status": "ill_posed",
      "unique": false,
      "stable": false,
      "ill_posed": true,
      "requires_prior": true,
      "difficulty": "high",
      "note": "INTERPRETATION — Finite survey geometry and noise leave an unstable, non-unique spatial inverse. Regularisation selects a model."
    },
    "evidence_type": "accepted_constitutive_theory",
    "references": [
      "li1996",
      "astic2020",
      "blakely1995"
    ],
    "modality": [
      "magnetic"
    ],
    "notes": "DERIVATION — Factorisation of the susceptibility map (Li 1996, eq. 1) into $\\\\mathbf M=\\\\chi\\\\mathbf H_0$ followed by this operator; vector version used by Astic 2020, SS50–51. The kernel is the standard magnetostatic dipole kernel (Blakely 1995, ch. 4–5). The ambient field enters twice: as the magnitude in $\\\\mathbf M=\\\\chi\\\\mathbf H_0$ and as the projection direction here. Gradient-tensor or component data would use a different projection and are not distinguished by the single magnetic_anomaly node. The vector inverse (MVI) has three unknowns per cell against one scalar datum per station, so it is more underdetermined than the susceptibility inverse."
  }

